WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.379
Evidence collection tools help preserve information before it disappears or changes.

00:00:05.379 --> 00:00:15.493
Disk imaging tools can capture a full storage device or a targeted logical collection, depending on the investigation need and operational constraints.

00:00:15.493 --> 00:00:26.897
Full images may be useful when deeper forensic analysis is expected, while targeted collections may be more practical when speed, storage, or business impact matters.

00:00:26.897 --> 00:00:33.568
The choice should be documented so later reviewers understand the limits of the evidence.

00:00:33.568 --> 00:00:36.795
Memory acquisition is another important capability.

00:00:36.795 --> 00:00:45.618
Memory can contain running processes, command lines, network connections, loaded modules, encryption keys, injected code, and other volatile artifacts.

00:00:45.618 --> 00:00:51.428
It can also disappear when a system is shut down or rebooted.

00:00:51.428 --> 00:01:01.971
A toolkit should include tested, approved methods for collecting memory when appropriate, along with guidance on when collection is worth the operational risk.

00:01:01.971 --> 00:01:07.351
Log export and file collection are often the backbone of investigations.

00:01:07.351 --> 00:01:20.046
Responders may need Windows Event Logs, Linux logs, EDR exports, authentication events, firewall records, proxy logs, DNS data, email evidence, browser artifacts, cloud audit records, and application logs.

00:01:20.046 --> 00:01:29.729
The toolkit should include procedures that explain how to collect these sources, where to store them, and what metadata to record.

00:01:29.729 --> 00:01:41.994
Screenshots and notes can also be evidence, especially when they capture console state, security tool findings, user reports, or administrative changes that may not be exported cleanly.

00:01:41.994 --> 00:01:46.944
Collection should preserve integrity and minimize unnecessary changes to systems.

00:01:46.944 --> 00:01:59.639
That means using working copies where possible, avoiding casual browsing through evidence, recording commands or tools used, and keeping collection activity separate from attacker activity in the timeline.
